WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.935
Orleans.Lattice, in three minutes: why it
exists, what it is, and where to start.

2
00:00:07.935 --> 00:00:11.930
Almost every application has
to remember things: what is in a basket,

3
00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:14.941
who may open a document,
how often a page is viewed.

4
00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:17.134
Developers call that memory state.

5
00:00:18.134 --> 00:00:20.991
Many applications run
in more than one place at once,

6
00:00:20.991 --> 00:00:23.309
so that they keep going
if one place fails.

7
00:00:23.659 --> 00:00:27.569
Then two places can change the same thing
at the same moment.

8
00:00:27.919 --> 00:00:31.210
Usually, changes take turns,
with a lock or a vote,

9
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.791
and memory lives in a separate database.

10
00:00:34.141 --> 00:00:36.456
Orleans.Lattice takes
a different approach.

11
00:00:36.456 --> 00:00:39.201
It keeps the working memory
inside the application.

12
00:00:39.551 --> 00:00:43.625
And it records each change so
that every place reaches the same answer,

13
00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:45.748
in whatever order the changes arrive.

14
00:00:45.748 --> 00:00:47.068
Neither waits its turn.

15
00:00:47.418 --> 00:00:49.791
It runs on one machine
with no cloud account,

16
00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:52.112
and grows to many regions
without a rewrite.

17
00:00:53.112 --> 00:00:57.563
In technical terms, Orleans.Lattice
is a platform for building durable,

18
00:00:57.563 --> 00:01:00.510
distributed state systems
on Microsoft Orleans.

19
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.162
At its centre is a sorted key-value store
inside your own cluster:

20
00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:07.964
no external database,
coordinator or queue.

21
00:01:08.314 --> 00:01:09.896
It takes three positions.

22
00:01:12.096 --> 00:01:17.776
First: state lives in grains, in the same
cluster as the code that uses it.

23
00:01:18.126 --> 00:01:19.552
A read is a grain call,

24
00:01:19.552 --> 00:01:24.451
not a round trip to a separate database
tier with its own scaling and failures.

25
00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:28.489
Second: conflict resolution is algebraic.

26
00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:32.448
Cluster A adds three,
cluster B concurrently adds five,

27
00:01:32.448 --> 00:01:33.892
each to its own count.

28
00:01:33.892 --> 00:01:36.057
Neither state is above the other.

29
00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:40.631
Each merges the other's update
by taking the larger value per replica,

30
00:01:40.631 --> 00:01:43.347
so both arrive at the same state:
their join.

31
00:01:43.697 --> 00:01:46.581
Deliver that update twice,
and nothing changes.

32
00:01:46.931 --> 00:01:50.937
Merges are commutative,
associative and idempotent.

33
00:01:51.287 --> 00:01:54.075
So any cluster can accept
a write to any key,

34
00:01:54.075 --> 00:01:57.111
with no lock manager
and no consensus round trip.

35
00:01:57.111 --> 00:01:59.527
For plain values, the last writer wins.

36
00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:02.601
Third: everything else is a seam.

37
00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:06.246
Storage, identity, governance,
replication, administration

38
00:02:06.246 --> 00:02:10.331
and observability are companion
packages behind documented seams.

39
00:02:10.681 --> 00:02:15.179
A host takes only what it registers, and
a capability it leaves out costs nothing.

40
00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:18.632
Start local: one machine,
no cloud account.

41
00:02:18.982 --> 00:02:23.015
Add a team: identity,
authorization, schemas and tenants.

42
00:02:23.365 --> 00:02:28.487
Go global: active in every region at once,
with backup and an autoscaling signal.

43
00:02:28.837 --> 00:02:31.939
At every stage,
the programming model is the same.

44
00:02:31.939 --> 00:02:34.544
Your code resolves ILattice, and calls it.

45
00:02:35.544 --> 00:02:38.030
The documentation has three ways in.

46
00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:43.693
Build, if you are writing code against
ILattice: start with Hello, Lattice.

47
00:02:44.043 --> 00:02:49.376
Evaluate, if you are deciding whether
it fits: start with When Lattice fits,

48
00:02:49.376 --> 00:02:50.779
and when it doesn't.

49
00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.478
Operate, if you are running an estate.

50
00:02:53.478 --> 00:02:54.158
You choose.
